# Summary
Introduced a release-integrity bypass / binary planting issue in the new embedded Spout helper flow. The default helper path is eligible for embedded fallback, but the resolver returns an executable beside the app before trying the embedded helper, and later code executes the returned path.
This commit changes the primary release artifact to a signed single executable with embedded `spout-capture.exe` and `SpoutLibrary.dll`. However, `ResolveSpoutHelperPath` still checks for `spout-capture.exe` beside the main executable first and returns it before using the embedded helper. As a result, a user can verify the PGP signature of `ClipForVRChat-vX.Y.Z-windows-amd64.exe`, place or already have an attacker-controlled `spout-capture.exe` in the same directory, and the app will execute that unsigned adjacent helper when the Spout helper check, sender refresh, or Stream capture path is used. This defeats the security benefit of embedding the helper in the signed single exe. The external helper should only be used when explicitly configured, or when the build has no embedded helper available; for the default helper name in an embedded build, the embedded asset should be preferred over an adjacent file.

